exports[`renderMapReplyMarkdown > renders the recorded rent-control reply 1`] = `
"**Argumend map: Does rent control end up hurting the renters it is meant to help?**
The map's claim: Rent control ends up hurting renters as a group: the protection it gives tenants who hold a capped lease is outweighed by the rentals it takes off the market and the higher rents and longer searches it leaves everyone else.

The largest share of this thread (4 of 8 turns) is arguing about **The rental supply**. 1 of 8 turns was not an argument about the topic. Some turns argue about different sections of the map as if they were one disagreement. A key term is being used to mean different things.

**Pattern:** Mixed disagreement.

**The crux for this section:** The Rental Supply Response Test. Count both sides of the rental ledger under a cap: new rentals built, and existing rentals lost to condo conversion, owner move-in, demolition or withdrawal. A cap that exempts new construction can still shrink the stock if owners of covered buildings sell, convert or let units sit. The test applies to each design separately: Oregon's and California's 2019 laws (new buildings exempt for 15 years), St. Paul's 3% cap before and after it exempted new construction, Berlin's 2020 freeze, and New York's 2026 freeze.

**Strongest evidence in this section:**
- Supports the map's claim: San Francisco 1994: covered landlords cut rental supply 15%, raising citywide rents an estimated 5.1% (Diamond, McQuade & Qian). Diamond, McQuade & Qian, NBER Working Paper 24181 (2018); published in the American Economic Review 109(9): 3365-94 (2019)
- Cuts against the map's claim: New Jersey's moderate local caps showed no significant effect on rents, quality or values in 2000-2010 Census comparisons (Ambrosius et al. 2015). Ambrosius, Gilderbloom, Steele, Meares & Keating, 'Forty years of rent control,' Cities 49: 121-133 (2015), as described in Hockert, 'Rent Control Capitalization into Land Values' (working paper, 30 June 2026)

**Cruxes this thread touched:** The Rental Supply Response Test | The Bridge-or-Delay Test
**Never reached:** Neighborhood stability and access for new renters

Full map: https://argumend.org/topics/rent-control-effectiveness"
